{0 Building a Raycasting Engine from Scratch}

A step-by-step tutorial for rebuilding this engine by hand, in OCaml on SDL2.
Each {b phase} ends in something you can run and see, and each {b step} adds one
idea. Read the concept, write the module, check the checkpoint, move on.

By the end you will have: a world of rooms joined at doorways you see through and
walk through, walls at any angle carrying their own heights and materials, an
inclined floor and either an inclined ceiling or an open sky, mouse look with
pitch, a resizable and fullscreen window, see-through walls, wall decals and
billboarded sprites — all drawn by a small software renderer you wrote.

If instead you want to {e use} this engine rather than rebuild it, the other page
is the one you want: {{!page-"making-a-game"} Making a game on CamlCast}.

{1 Before you start}

{b What you need to know.} Basic OCaml — modules, records, variants, [let]. A
little trigonometry. No graphics background is assumed: every projection here is
derived from similar triangles, in full, where it is first used.

{b What you need installed.} An OCaml toolchain and the SDL2 bindings. SDL2
itself is a system library, so it is installed by your package manager rather
than by opam, and that is the only step that differs per platform.

{@sh[
# Debian or Ubuntu — opam resolves the system packages itself
sudo apt install libsdl2-dev libsdl2-image-dev

# macOS — Homebrew ships sdl2-compat under the name sdl2, which opam cannot
# detect, so it has to be told to stop looking
brew install sdl2 sdl2_image

# Windows — from the MSYS2 mingw64 shell
pacman -S mingw-w64-x86_64-SDL2 mingw-w64-x86_64-SDL2_image
]}

Then, in an empty directory:

{@sh[
opam switch create . 5.5.0
eval $(opam env)
opam install dune tsdl        # add --no-depexts on macOS
dune --version                # you should get 3.21 or later
]}

{b How to read this page.} Each {b phase} ends in something you can run and look
at; each {b step} adds one idea. Every step has the same shape:

{ol
{li {b the concept} — what is being built and why, with the derivation;}
{li {b the code} — which file it goes in, named above the block;}
{li {b a checkpoint} — what you should see, and what it means if you do not.}}

Do not skip the checkpoints. A raycaster fails silently: a sign error gives you a
black screen rather than an exception, and it is far easier to find the sign in
the twenty lines you just wrote than in the four hundred you have by Phase 6.

{b Guiding principles} worth keeping as you go: small self-contained modules,
readable maths with the derivation written down, and the [Result] monad instead
of a staircase of error matches.

{1 Words used here}

Worth having in hand before Phase 1, since most of them appear without ceremony.

{ul
{li {b column} — one vertical line of screen pixels. A raycaster shoots one ray
    per column, so "per column" and "per ray" mean the same thing.}
{li {b texel} — one pixel of a texture, addressed by [u] across and [v] down, to
    keep it distinct from a pixel of the screen.}
{li {b cell} / {b world unit} — the unit the world is measured in. The eye sits
    half a unit up, and a texture tiles once per unit.}
{li {b horizon} — the screen row the eye looks along. Level, it is the middle
    row; pitch slides it away from the middle.}
{li {b azimuth} — a compass direction as an angle, used for the sky.}
{li {b threshold} / {b doorway} — a gap in a room's wall loop that another room
    is joined to.}
{li {b jamb} — the piece of wall left standing either side of a doorway.}
{li {b lintel} — the strip of wall left standing above a doorway.}
{li {b portal} — an open threshold, through which the neighbouring room is drawn
    recursively.}
{li {b billboard} — a flat picture that always turns to face the player, which is
    how a sprite is drawn.}
{li {b Hor+} — a way of handling window resizes in which the vertical field of
    view is fixed, so a wider window shows more world to the sides rather than a
    magnified view.}}

{1 Table of contents}

{ul
{li {b Phase 0 — Setup}: project, window, game loop, the [Result] monad.}
{li {b Phase 1 — Foundations}: vectors, and the core raycasting idea.}
{li {b Phase 2 — A first 3D view}: segments, ray casting, the camera, a framebuffer, solid walls, movement.}
{li {b Phase 3 — Making walls look real}: colour, shading, fog, textures, variable heights.}
{li {b Phase 4 — Floor, ceiling, sky}: inclined planes, per-pixel casting, textured planes, the sky.}
{li {b Phase 5 — Camera & window}: mouse look, pitch, resizing, fullscreen.}
{li {b Phase 6 — Props}: a per-pixel depth buffer, transparent walls, decals, sprites.}
{li {b Phase 7 — Rooms and doorways}: rigid transforms, portals, and a world that is a graph of rooms.}
{li {b Phase 8 — Assembly}: the engine loop, resource safety, tests, docs.}
{li {b Appendix}: module map, constants, hard-won gotchas, exercises.}
}

{1 Phase 0 — Setup}

{2 Step 0.1 — The toolchain and project layout}

The toolchain is installed by now — see {b Before you start} above. What is left
is the shape of the project. One library holding the engine, one executable to
run it, and a test directory that stays empty until Phase 8.

{[
camlcast/
├── dune-project
├── lib/          # the engine, one module per file
│   └── dune
├── bin/          # the executable entry point
│   ├── dune
│   └── main.ml
└── test/         # unit tests (added in Phase 8)
]}

[dune-project]:

{[
(lang dune 3.21)

(package
 (name camlcast)
 (depends
  (ocaml (>= 5.1))
  tsdl
  (alcotest :with-test)))
]}

[ocaml >= 5.1] is for [Array.find_index], which Phase 7 uses to resolve a room's
name to its index. Add [tsdl-image] when you want to read a PNG or JPEG rather
than generate every pattern in code; nothing below needs it.

[lib/dune] — a {b public name} matters later so [odoc] will document it:

{[
(library (name camlcast) (public_name camlcast) (libraries tsdl))
]}

[bin/dune]:

{[
(executable (name main) (libraries camlcast))
]}

{b Checkpoint.} [dune build] finishes silently. It has nothing to compile yet,
which is the point: if it complains here it is about the toolchain and not about
anything you have written.

{b A note on this repository.} Its own executable is [bin/demo.ml], installed as
[camlcast-demo], because it is a launcher for twenty-odd demos rather than one
game. Yours is [bin/main.ml] and stays that way.

{2 Step 0.2 — The [Result] monad}

Every SDL call can fail ([('a, [ `Msg of string ]) result]). Rather than nest
[match]es, define binding operators once ([lib/result_ext.ml]):

{@ocaml[
let ( let* ) = Result.bind                 (* chain: stop at the first error *)
let ( let+ ) result f = Result.map f result
]}

Now [let* x = fallible () in ...] reads like normal code but short-circuits on
error.

{2 Step 0.3 — Config: constants in one place}

Put every tunable number in [lib/config.ml] so the rest of the code reads as
logic, not magic numbers. We will fill it as we go; start with:

{@ocaml[
let window_title = "CamlCast"
let initial_width = 1024 and initial_height = 768
let fov = Float.pi /. 3.          (* 60°, horizontal, at reference_aspect *)
let reference_aspect = 4. /. 3.   (* the shape fov is quoted for *)
let frame_budget = 1. /. 60.      (* seconds a frame may take ≈ 60 FPS *)
]}

{2 Step 0.4 — A window and a game loop}

Get a blank window that stays open until you press Escape. This also introduces
[with_resource], the pattern we use so every SDL resource is freed even on error
([Fun.protect] runs the finaliser no matter what).

{@ocaml[
open Tsdl
open Result_ext

let with_resource acquire release use =
  let* r = acquire () in
  Fun.protect ~finally:(fun () -> release r) (fun () -> use r)

let rec loop window event =
  (* Draining the event queue every frame is mandatory or the OS thinks the
     app hung. *)
  let quit = ref false in
  while Sdl.poll_event (Some event) do
    match Sdl.Event.(enum (get event typ)) with
    | `Quit -> quit := true
    | `Key_down when Sdl.Event.(get event keyboard_scancode) = Sdl.Scancode.escape -> quit := true
    | _ -> ()
  done;
  if !quit then Ok ()
  (* A flat sleep will do until Phase 8, which measures the frame instead. *)
  else (Sdl.delay 16l; loop window event)

let run () =
  with_resource (fun () -> Sdl.init Sdl.Init.(video + events)) (fun () -> Sdl.quit ())
  @@ fun () ->
  with_resource
    (fun () -> Sdl.create_window Config.window_title
                 ~w:Config.initial_width ~h:Config.initial_height Sdl.Window.(shown + resizable))
    Sdl.destroy_window
  @@ fun window -> loop window (Sdl.Event.create ())
]}

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 0.} [dune exec bin/main.exe] opens a black resizable
window that closes on Escape. From here on, "run it" means this.

{b If nothing opens}, the error is an SDL one and [run] returned it rather than
raising: print it. If the window opens and then hangs with a spinning cursor, the
event queue is not being drained — that [while Sdl.poll_event] loop is not
optional, and an OS that gets no answer decides the program has died.

{1 Phase 1 — Foundations}

{2 Step 1.1 — Vectors}

The whole world is two-dimensional and flat; the 3-D look is an illusion we
produce later by choosing how tall to draw each wall. [lib/vec.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type t = { x : float; y : float }

let make x y = { x; y }
let add a b = { x = a.x +. b.x; y = a.y +. b.y }
let sub a b = { x = a.x -. b.x; y = a.y -. b.y }
let scale v k = { x = v.x *. k; y = v.y *. k }
let length v = Float.hypot v.x v.y
let dot a b = (a.x *. b.x) +. (a.y *. b.y)

(* The 2-D cross product is a scalar (signed parallelogram area). It is zero
   exactly when the vectors are parallel — the heart of ray-vs-wall tests. *)
let cross a b = (a.x *. b.y) -. (a.y *. b.x)

let normalize v = let l = length v in if l = 0. then v else scale v (1. /. l)

(* 0 rad points along +x; angle grows clockwise on screen because y points down. *)
let of_angle a = { x = cos a; y = sin a }
let rotate v a = let c = cos a and s = sin a in
  { x = (v.x *. c) -. (v.y *. s); y = (v.x *. s) +. (v.y *. c) }

(* Perpendicular, i.e. a quarter turn — exact and cheaper than rotate by π/2. *)
let perp v = { x = -.v.y; y = v.x }
]}

{2 Step 1.2 — The idea of raycasting}

The plan: for every {b vertical column of pixels} on screen, shoot {b one ray}
from the player across the flat world, find the first wall it hits, and draw a
vertical strip whose {b height encodes the distance} — near walls are tall, far
walls are short. Do that for all columns and the flat world looks 3-D.

Two facts make this cheap and correct:

{b The camera plane.} We don't rotate a ray per column with trigonometry.
Instead we keep two unit vectors: [dir] (where the player looks) and [right] (a
quarter-turn from it). The ray for a column is

{[
ray_direction = dir + right * camera_x
]}

where [camera_x] runs from [-half_width] at the left edge of the screen to
[+half_width] at the right. [half_width] encodes the field of view. All columns
share [dir]; only the [right] term changes — one multiply and one add per
column.

{b Why distances have no fish-eye.} We measure the hit distance [t] in *units of
that (un-normalised) [ray_direction]*. Project the hit onto the view direction:

{[
(t · ray_direction) · dir = t·(dir·dir) + t·camera_x·(right·dir)
                          = t·1        + t·camera_x·0            = t
]}

So [t] {e is} the distance measured {b perpendicular to the camera plane} — not
the straight-line distance to the player. That perpendicular distance is exactly
what a flat projection needs; using the true Euclidean distance instead makes
walls bulge toward you at the screen edges (the classic fish-eye), and this
formulation removes it with no [cos] correction anywhere.

{b Aside — grids vs segments.} Classic raycasters (Wolfenstein 3D) put walls on a grid and step the ray cell-to-cell with the DDA algorithm. We instead let walls be {b arbitrary line segments}, so rooms can be any shape. The projection maths above is identical either way; only {e how we find [t]} differs. We use segments from the start.

{1 Phase 2 — A first 3D view (solid walls)}

{2 Step 2.1 — A room as wall segments}

A level is a set of wall segments, plus (later) a floor, a ceiling and sprites.
Call it a {b room}, because that is what it is: Phase 7 introduces a [World] as a
graph of these, and starting with the right name saves a rename later.
[lib/room.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type wall = {
  a : Vec.t; b : Vec.t;        (* endpoints *)
  height : float;              (* how far it rises above the floor, in cells *)
  edge : Vec.t;                (* b - a, precomputed *)
  length : float;              (* |b - a| *)
  normal : Vec.t;              (* unit perpendicular, for shading later *)
}

type t = { walls : wall array; spawn : Vec.t }

let wall ~height a b =
  let edge = Vec.sub b a in
  { a; b; height; edge; length = Vec.length edge;
    normal = Vec.normalize (Vec.perp edge) }

let make ~spawn walls = { walls = Array.of_list walls; spawn }

(* A hand-checkable 4×4 room: from the centre every wall is 2 cells away. *)
let default =
  make ~spawn:(Vec.make 2. 2.)
    [ wall ~height:3. (Vec.make 0. 0.) (Vec.make 4. 0.);
      wall ~height:3. (Vec.make 4. 0.) (Vec.make 4. 4.);
      wall ~height:3. (Vec.make 4. 4.) (Vec.make 0. 4.);
      wall ~height:3. (Vec.make 0. 4.) (Vec.make 0. 0.) ]
]}

Three things are precomputed on every wall because the ray test wants all three
and there is no reason to derive them per ray: the edge vector, its length, and
the unit normal. {b Wind the boundary counterclockwise} and every normal points
into the room, which is what the shading in Phase 3 assumes.

Walls are plain for now. Phase 3 gives each one a [material] and that is the only
change this record needs.

{2 Step 2.2 — Ray–segment intersection}

Write the ray as [origin + t·direction] and a wall as [a + s·edge] with
[edge = b − a]. Setting them equal and solving with the cross product:

{[
denom = direction × edge
t     = (a − origin) × edge      / denom
s     = (a − origin) × direction / denom
]}

The ray meets the wall when [denom ≠ 0] (not parallel), [t > 0] (ahead), and
[s ∈ [0,1]] (between the endpoints). Because we will later want to see {e over} short
walls, we keep {b every} wall the ray crosses, sorted {b farthest-first} (the
order we will paint them). [lib/ray.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type hit = { distance : float; along : float; wall : Room.wall }
let min_distance = 1e-4          (* so standing on a wall can't divide by zero *)

let cast (room : Room.t) ~(origin : Vec.t) ~(direction : Vec.t) =
  let hits =
    Array.fold_left (fun acc (w : Room.wall) ->
      let denom = Vec.cross direction w.edge in
      if Float.abs denom < 1e-12 then acc              (* parallel *)
      else
        let ao = Vec.sub w.a origin in
        let t = Vec.cross ao w.edge /. denom in
        let s = Vec.cross ao direction /. denom in
        if t > min_distance && s >= 0. && s <= 1.
        then { distance = t; along = s *. w.length; wall = w } :: acc
        else acc)
      [] room.walls
  in
  List.sort (fun h1 h2 -> Float.compare h2.distance h1.distance) hits

let nearest hits =           (* the closest wall, if any *)
  List.fold_left (fun best h -> match best with
    | Some b when b.distance <= h.distance -> best | _ -> Some h) None hits
]}

[along] is the world distance from [a] to the hit — we will thread textures
across the wall with it.

{2 Step 2.3 — The player and the viewport}

The camera pose ([lib/player.ml]). Store [dir] and [right] as unit vectors so
turning stays exact; [pitch] (look up/down) comes in Phase 5.

{@ocaml[
type t = { pos : Vec.t; dir : Vec.t; right : Vec.t; pitch : float }
let create ~pos ~angle =
  let dir = Vec.of_angle angle in { pos; dir; right = Vec.perp dir; pitch = 0. }
let spawn room = create ~pos:room.Room.spawn ~angle:0.
let turn p ~radians =
  { p with dir = Vec.rotate p.dir radians; right = Vec.rotate p.right radians }
]}

The {b viewport} turns the current window size into camera geometry
([lib/viewport.ml]). This is where the field of view and the projection live.

Two rules decide the geometry. Let [projection] be *the number of screen pixels
one world unit covers at distance 1*.

{ol
{li {b Pixels stay square.} A one-cell-wide, one-cell-tall wall must cover the same pixels horizontally and vertically. Vertically it covers [projection/d]. Horizontally the camera plane spans [2·half_width] units at distance 1 across [width] pixels, so a unit at distance [d] covers [width/(2·half_width·d)]. Equate them: [half_width = width / (2·projection)].}
{li {b Anchor the vertical field of view.} Set [projection] from the window {b height}, so the vertical FOV is fixed and a wider window shows {e more world} to the sides (Hor+), not a magnified view.}
}

{@ocaml[
type t = { width : int; height : int; half_width : float; projection : float;
           eye_z : float; horizon : float }

let vertical_half_extent = Float.tan (Config.fov /. 2.) /. Config.reference_aspect

let create ~pitch ~eye_z ~width ~height =
  let width = Int.max 1 width and height = Int.max 1 height in
  let projection = float_of_int height /. 2. /. vertical_half_extent in
  { width; height; projection; eye_z;
    half_width = float_of_int width /. 2. /. projection;
    (* Level, the horizon is the middle row; pitch shears it (Phase 5). *)
    horizon = (float_of_int height /. 2.) +. (pitch *. float_of_int height) }

(* The ray for a column: camera_x runs -1 (left) .. +1 (right). *)
let ray_direction t (player : Player.t) ~column =
  let camera_x = (2. *. float_of_int column /. float_of_int t.width) -. 1. in
  Vec.add player.dir (Vec.scale player.right (t.half_width *. camera_x))
]}

{b Projection formula.} A point of world height [z] at perpendicular distance
[d], with the eye at elevation [eye_z], projects to the screen row

{[
screen_y = horizon − projection · (z − eye_z) / d
]}

(similar triangles: its height above the eye shrinks with distance and is
measured down from the horizon). Add it:

{@ocaml[
let project_height t ~z ~distance =
  t.horizon -. (t.projection *. (z -. t.eye_z) /. distance)
let row_factor t ~row = (float_of_int row -. t.horizon) /. t.projection
]}

{2 Step 2.4 — A software framebuffer}

We render into a CPU pixel buffer and upload it once per frame as a streaming
texture, then let the GPU scale it to the window. (A software buffer is what
makes per-pixel floor casting and blending possible later.) [lib/framebuffer.ml]:

{@ocaml[
open Tsdl
open Result_ext

type t = {
  texture : Sdl.texture;
  pixels : (int, Bigarray.int8_unsigned_elt, Bigarray.c_layout) Bigarray.Array1.t;
  depth : float array;                 (* per-pixel depth, used in Phase 6 *)
  width : int; height : int;
}

(* We write the bytes as B, G, R, A. SDL names a packed format from the most
   significant byte down, so that order is what ARGB8888 means on a
   little-endian machine and what BGRA8888 means on a big-endian one. *)
let pixel_format =
  if Sys.big_endian then Sdl.Pixel.format_bgra8888 else Sdl.Pixel.format_argb8888

let create sdl ~width ~height =
  let+ texture = Sdl.create_texture sdl pixel_format
                   Sdl.Texture.access_streaming ~w:width ~h:height in
  { texture;
    pixels = Bigarray.(Array1.create int8_unsigned c_layout (width * height * 4));
    depth = Array.make (width * height) infinity; width; height }

(* Writing per channel avoids boxing an int32 in the hot loop. *)
let set t ~x ~y ~r ~g ~b =
  let i = ((y * t.width) + x) * 4 and p = t.pixels in
  Bigarray.Array1.unsafe_set p i b;
  Bigarray.Array1.unsafe_set p (i+1) g;
  Bigarray.Array1.unsafe_set p (i+2) r;
  Bigarray.Array1.unsafe_set p (i+3) 255

let clear_depth t = Array.fill t.depth 0 (Array.length t.depth) infinity

let present sdl t ~dst =
  (* update_texture's pitch is in BYTES here (4 per pixel). *)
  let* () = Sdl.update_texture t.texture None t.pixels (t.width * 4) in
  Sdl.render_copy sdl t.texture ~dst
]}

{b Gotcha.} [update_texture] for a {e streaming} texture wants pitch in {b bytes}; a {e static}-texture upload wants pitch in {b elements}. Mixing these up squashes or stripes the image. Likewise [render_read_pixels] wants bytes.

{2 Step 2.5 — Draw solid walls}

For each column: cast the ray, take the nearest hit, and fill the wall's strip.
The wall stands from [floor_z] (0 for now — flat floor) to [floor_z + height].
Project foot and top with [project_height] (eye at [Config.eye_height = 0.5], the
middle of a one-cell wall). Before the real floor casting of Phase 4, just fill
ceiling and floor with flat colours. [lib/renderer.ml] (first cut):

{@ocaml[
let draw_frame fb room (player : Player.t) =
  let width = fb.Framebuffer.width and height = fb.Framebuffer.height in
  let viewport = Viewport.create ~pitch:0. ~eye_z:Config.eye_height ~width ~height in
  for column = 0 to width - 1 do
    (* placeholder background: top half ceiling, bottom half floor *)
    for y = 0 to height - 1 do
      if y < height / 2 then Framebuffer.set fb ~x:column ~y ~r:40 ~g:42 ~b:54
      else Framebuffer.set fb ~x:column ~y ~r:68 ~g:71 ~b:90
    done;
    let dir = Viewport.ray_direction viewport player ~column in
    match Ray.(nearest (cast room ~origin:player.pos ~direction:dir)) with
    | None -> ()
    | Some hit ->
      let d = hit.Ray.distance in
      let y_foot = Viewport.project_height viewport ~z:0. ~distance:d in
      let y_top  = Viewport.project_height viewport ~z:hit.Ray.wall.Room.height ~distance:d in
      let first = Int.max 0 (int_of_float y_top)
      and last  = Int.min (height - 1) (int_of_float y_foot) in
      for y = first to last do Framebuffer.set fb ~x:column ~y ~r:180 ~g:120 ~b:120 done
  done
]}

Wire this into the loop: each frame ask SDL the output size, (re)size the
framebuffer to match, [draw_frame], [present], [render_present]. (We'll flesh
the loop out in Phase 8; for now a fixed-size framebuffer created once is fine.)

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 2.} You are standing in a shaded room. Walls are solid
strips, tall up close and short far away, with no fish-eye. It already looks 3-D.

{{image:images/flat.png}Three untextured pillars of falling height at rising
distance, in a room with no floor drawn yet}

That picture is the finished engine dressed back down to this point — flat
materials, and a floor and ceiling of one dark colour standing in for the two
you have not written yet. Everything in it that matters here is what your own
code is producing: the strips, their shading, and the fact that the near pillar
is taller on screen than the far one without either being drawn any differently.

{b If the screen is one flat colour}, no ray is hitting anything: check that
[t > min_distance] and that [s] is being tested against [0..1]. {b If the walls
bulge towards you at the screen edges}, a Euclidean distance has crept in
somewhere — the whole point of measuring [t] in units of the un-normalised ray
direction is that it is already perpendicular. {b If the room is inside out},
the boundary is wound clockwise.

{2 Step 2.6 — Movement with wall sliding}

Move along [dir] (forward) and [right] (strafe), resolving each axis
independently so hitting a wall at an angle keeps the free component — you slide
along instead of sticking. Collision treats the player as a small disc of radius
[Config.collision_padding] and, crucially, tests the {b whole path} and not just
its endpoint: the step sweeps that disc from [from] to [dest], so a fast step can
neither tunnel through a thin wall nor clip past the end of one. That is one
question — how close do the segments [from..dest] and [a..b] come? In [Room]:

{@ocaml[
let distance_to_segment (p : Vec.t) ~a ~b =
  let edge = Vec.sub b a in
  let length2 = Vec.dot edge edge in
  if length2 = 0. then Vec.length (Vec.sub p a)
  else
    let s = Float.max 0. (Float.min 1. (Vec.dot (Vec.sub p a) edge /. length2)) in
    Vec.length (Vec.sub p (Vec.add a (Vec.scale edge s)))

let distance_to_wall (w : wall) p = distance_to_segment p ~a:w.a ~b:w.b

let blocked t p =                      (* is standing at p legal at all? *)
  Array.exists (fun w -> distance_to_wall w p < Config.collision_padding) t.walls

let segments_cross a1 a2 b1 b2 =       (* same cross-product test as Ray *)
  let d1 = Vec.sub a2 a1 and d2 = Vec.sub b2 b1 in
  let denom = Vec.cross d1 d2 in
  if Float.abs denom < 1e-12 then false
  else let off = Vec.sub b1 a1 in
    let t = Vec.cross off d2 /. denom and u = Vec.cross off d1 /. denom in
    t >= 0. && t <= 1. && u >= 0. && u <= 1.

(* Segments that cross are zero apart; two that miss are closest at an endpoint
   of one of them, so four point-to-segment distances settle it. *)
let distance_between_segments a1 a2 b1 b2 =
  if segments_cross a1 a2 b1 b2 then 0.
  else
    let to_b p = distance_to_segment p ~a:b1 ~b:b2
    and to_a p = distance_to_segment p ~a:a1 ~b:a2 in
    Float.min (Float.min (to_b a1) (to_b a2)) (Float.min (to_a b1) (to_a b2))

let passable t ~from ~dest =
  not (Array.exists
         (fun w -> distance_between_segments from dest w.a w.b
                   < Config.collision_padding)
         t.walls)
]}

In [Player]:

{@ocaml[
let slide room player (delta : Vec.t) =
  let open Vec in
  let step from moved = if Room.passable room ~from ~dest:moved then moved else from in
  let after_x = step player.pos { player.pos with x = player.pos.x +. delta.x } in
  let after_y = step after_x   { after_x   with y = after_x.y   +. delta.y } in
  { player with pos = after_y }

let walk room player ~forward ~strafe =
  slide room player (Vec.add (Vec.scale player.dir forward)
                              (Vec.scale player.right strafe))
]}

Read the keyboard as a {e state snapshot} (held keys move continuously) and feed
[walk]/[turn] in the loop. We build the full [Input] module in Phase 5.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 2.6.} You can walk around the room and slide along
walls, and cannot get through one by running at it.

{b If you stick in corners}, the two axes are being resolved together rather than
one at a time. {b If a fast step tunnels through a thin wall}, the test is on the
endpoint rather than on the swept path.

{1 Phase 3 — Making walls look real}

{2 Step 3.1 — Colour, shading, fog}

[lib/color.ml] — 8-bit RGB with a couple of operations:

{@ocaml[
type t = { r : int; g : int; b : int }
let rgb r g b = { r; g; b }
let clamp_channel v = Int.max 0 (Int.min 255 v)
let shade c f = let a v = clamp_channel (int_of_float (float_of_int v *. f)) in
  { r = a c.r; g = a c.g; b = a c.b }
let lerp a b t =                          (* the distance fade below, and the sky later *)
  let m x y = clamp_channel (int_of_float ((float_of_int x *. (1.-.t)) +. (float_of_int y *. t))) in
  { r = m a.r b.r; g = m a.g b.g; b = m a.b b.b }

(* Show a colour at a brightness on 0..255. Patterns are written in that scale,
   so this is the join between a pattern and the colour it is drawn in: it
   scales all three channels together, moving value without touching hue. *)
let level c v =
  let a x = clamp_channel (x * v / 255) in
  { r = a c.r; g = a c.g; b = a c.b }
]}

{3 The air, not a palette}

The obvious next move is a [Palette] module holding every colour the game uses
and an integer id on each wall to index it. Do not. It puts the whole look of a
game in one module that every level has to share, and an id nobody defined falls
through to a default grey instead of failing.

Put the colour {e on the wall} instead, and keep in one place only the things
that are genuinely properties of the {e world} rather than of any wall in it: how
it fades with distance, and where its light comes from. That is
[lib/atmosphere.ml] — the air a world is seen through.

Two dimmers make a flat world read as solid:

{ul
{li {b Distance fog}: full colour up close, blending towards [haze] as it
    recedes, until at [fog_distance] only [min_brightness] of the surface is
    left. {b Blending and not dimming}: what a surface loses to distance goes to
    the colour of the air, not to black. Cheap, but it gives strong depth — and
    it is what makes the haze a property worth having at all.}
{li {b Orientation shading}: dim a wall by how squarely its [normal] faces the
    light, in a band so nothing goes black. This one {e is} a multiply, because
    a wall turned away from the light goes dark rather than hazy. This is what
    makes corners visible.}}

{@ocaml[
type t = {
  haze : Color.t;          (* what distance fades to *)
  fog_distance : float;    (* the scale it fades over *)
  min_brightness : float;  (* the darkest a face ever gets *)
  light : Vec.t;           (* where the light comes from, unit *)
  ambient : float;         (* light arriving from everywhere *)
  directional : float;     (* light arriving from [light] *)
}

let make ~haze ~fog_distance ~min_brightness ~light ~ambient ~directional =
  { haze; fog_distance; min_brightness;
    light = Vec.normalize light; ambient; directional }

let fog t distance =
  Float.max t.min_brightness (1. -. (distance /. t.fog_distance))

let face_shading t n = t.ambient +. (t.directional *. Float.abs (Vec.dot n t.light))
]}

[ambient] and [directional] decide together how much a wall's angle matters: all
ambient and every wall is lit the same, all directional and a wall facing away
goes black. Those six numbers are most of what tells one place from another, and
a world carries exactly one of them.

Tint the wall strip with
[Color.lerp (Color.shade colour (face_shading air normal)) air.haze (1. -. fog air d)]
— where [colour] comes from the wall itself, which is the next step. Note which
factor goes where: the orientation multiplies the wall's own colour, and the fog
is the {e amount} of the blend towards the haze. Putting the fog inside the
[shade] as well would apply it twice.

{2 Step 3.2 — Wall textures}

A texture is a small square pattern, and {b a texel is a colour}. That is worth
stating because the tempting alternative — a texel as a {e brightness}, coloured
at draw time — cannot draw a wall with two colours in it. Brick with grey mortar
between it is not brick with pale red mortar between it, and a leaded window is
not tinted glass around tinted glass.

The reuse that a brightness-only texel would have got for free is bought back by
{b giving a pattern its colours as arguments, before [u] and [v]}. A partial
application is then a pattern, and one function serves any number of them.

Generate the pattern in code, which is what this tutorial does throughout and
what keeps it a pure testable function of [u] and [v]; [Texture.load] reads one
from a PNG or JPEG instead, once you have art to read. [lib/texture.ml]:

{@ocaml[
let size = 64                        (* what a generator makes unless told otherwise *)
type t = { size : int; texels : Color.t array; alpha : int array; opaque : bool }
let sample t ~u ~v = t.texels.((v * t.size) + u)

let generate f =                     (* a solid pattern from a colour fn *)
  { size;
    texels = Array.init (size*size) (fun i ->
      f ~u:(i mod size) ~v:(i / size));
    alpha = Array.make (size*size) 255; opaque = true }

(* Running-bond brick: 16-texel courses, every other shifted half a brick. The
   colours come first, so `brick ~color ~mortar` is itself a pattern. *)
let brick ~color ~mortar ~u ~v =
  let course = v / 16 in
  let u = (u + (if course land 1 = 0 then 0 else 16)) mod size in
  if v mod 16 < 2 || u mod 32 < 2 then Color.level mortar 210
  else Color.level color 225
(* ...panel, stone, checker, plain similarly... *)

(* Which texel column a hit at [offset] (0..1 across the face) falls in. *)
let column_of_offset off =
  Int.min (size-1) (Int.max 0 (int_of_float (off *. float_of_int size)))
]}

{3 What a wall is made of}

A wall should carry its pattern {e by value}, not an index into a table
somewhere. Wrap it, so that there is somewhere for a surface's other properties
to go as they arrive. [lib/material.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type t = { pattern : Texture.t }
let make ~pattern = { pattern }
let opaque t = t.pattern.Texture.opaque
]}

Now add [material : Material.t] to [Room.wall] and give [Room.wall] a
[~material] argument — the one change Phase 2's record needed:

{@ocaml[
let solid f = Material.make ~pattern:(Texture.generate f)
let red = solid (brick ~color:(Color.rgb 200 70 70) ~mortar:(Color.rgb 188 182 172))
let grey = solid (brick ~color:(Color.rgb 120 120 128) ~mortar:(Color.rgb 188 182 172))
]}

Two materials, one function, and the engine has no opinion about either: a
[Texture.generate] takes a function from a texel coordinate to a colour, and that
is the whole of the interface.

{b Texture mapping a strip.} Horizontally, [u] comes from the hit position along
the wall: [column_of_offset (frac hit.along)] — this tiles the pattern every
world unit. Vertically, for each pixel of the strip recover the world height it
looks at and take the fractional cell:

{@ocaml[
(* inside the per-pixel wall loop, distance d, wall foot at floor_z: *)
let z = viewport.eye_z -. ((float_of_int y -. viewport.horizon) *. d /. viewport.projection) in
let tile = let h = z -. floor_z in h -. Float.floor h in       (* 0 at cell bottom .. 1 at top *)
let v = int_of_float ((1. -. tile) *. float_of_int (Texture.size - 1)) in
(* The texel is already the colour. All that is left is the light on it: how
   squarely this wall faces the lamp multiplies it, and how far away it is
   blends it towards the colour of the air. *)
let texel = Texture.sample wall.Room.material.Material.pattern ~u ~v in
let lit = Color.lerp
    (Color.shade texel (Atmosphere.face_shading air wall.Room.normal))
    air.Atmosphere.haze
    (1. -. Atmosphere.fog air d) in
Framebuffer.set fb ~x:column ~y ~r:lit.Color.r ~g:lit.Color.g ~b:lit.Color.b
]}

Tiling per cell means a tall wall repeats its pattern instead of stretching it.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 3.2.} Textured, coloured, fogged walls, with the
mortar a colour of its own rather than a paler brick. Stand close to a course
line and check that. Then set [~haze] to something vivid — a strong blue — and
walk away from a wall: it should turn {e blue}, not black. If it darkens
instead, the fog is still a multiply.

{{image:images/masonry.png}A tiled green wall and an oak post, one pattern
function wearing two different colours}

{b If the pattern is stretched rather than tiled}, the [frac] is missing from the
horizontal lookup. {b If it is upside down}, that [1. -. tile] is why: [v] runs
down the texture and [z] runs up the world.

{2 Step 3.3 — Variable wall heights}

Each wall already has a [height]. Because the eye sits at [eye_z = 0.5], a wall
shorter than one cell drops below its neighbours and a taller one rises above
them. For that to {e read} correctly, a ray must {b not stop at the first wall} — a
low wall must not hide a tall one behind it. We already collect every hit,
farthest-first; now just paint them in that order (the {b painter's algorithm}):

{@ocaml[
let hits = Ray.cast room ~origin:player.pos ~direction:dir in
List.iter (fun hit -> draw_wall fb viewport room player ~column ~dir hit) hits
]}

A near wall paints over a far one; a tall wall still shows above a short one.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 3.3.} Short walls you can see over, with tall pillars
visible behind them.

{b If a low wall hides what is behind it}, the renderer is still stopping at the
nearest hit instead of painting every hit farthest-first.

{1 Phase 4 — Floor, ceiling, sky}

Now replace the flat ceiling/floor fills with real, textured, possibly
{b inclined} surfaces. This is the part that makes the renderer software.

{2 Step 4.1 — Inclined planes}

Model the floor (and ceiling) as a plane [z = a·x + b·y + c]. [lib/plane.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type t = { a : float; b : float; c : float }
let make ~a ~b ~c = { a; b; c }
let horizontal z = { a = 0.; b = 0.; c = z }
let elevation t (p : Vec.t) = (t.a *. p.x) +. (t.b *. p.y) +. t.c
let gradient t (dir : Vec.t) = (t.a *. dir.x) +. (t.b *. dir.y)   (* rise per unit along dir *)
]}

{b The casting equation.} For a pixel below the horizon in a column of direction
[dir], at what perpendicular distance [d] does the line of sight meet the plane?
The pixel sees, at distance [d], the world point [eye_pos + d·dir] at height
[eye_z − row_factor·d], where [row_factor = (row − horizon)/projection]. Set that
equal to the plane's own height there, [base + d·gradient] (with [base] the
plane under the eye), and solve:

{[
eye_z − row_factor·d = base + d·gradient
eye_z − base         = d·(row_factor + gradient)
d                    = (eye_z − base) / (row_factor + gradient)
]}

One formula serves both surfaces: the real one is whichever gives [d > 0].

{@ocaml[
let view_distance t ~eye_z ~eye_pos ~dir ~row_factor =
  let denom = row_factor +. gradient t dir in
  if Float.abs denom < 1e-9 then None
  else let d = (eye_z -. elevation t eye_pos) /. denom in
       if d > 0. then Some d else None
]}

Add [floor] and [ceiling] fields to [Room.t]. A surface is a plane and the
material covering it; a ceiling is either a roof or the open sky, which is a
{e variant} and not an option — [None] would say "no ceiling" when what is meant
is "something else overhead", and Step 4.4 has to draw that something.

{@ocaml[
type surface = { plane : Plane.t; material : Material.t }
type ceiling = Roof of surface | Open of Sky.t
]}

For a gentle wedge of a floor use e.g. [Plane.make ~a:0.06 ~b:0.03 ~c:0.].

{2 Step 4.2 — Casting the floor and ceiling per pixel}

Replace the placeholder background. For each column, precompute the two plane
gradients; for each row, cast the relevant plane (one division), get the world
point, colour it, fade by fog. [eye_z = floor_z(player) + eye_height].

{@ocaml[
let draw_planes fb viewport room air (player : Player.t) ~column ~dir =
  let open Viewport in
  let px = player.pos.x and py = player.pos.y and dx = dir.x and dy = dir.y in
  let floor_base = Plane.elevation room.floor player.pos in
  let gf = Plane.gradient room.floor dir in
  for y = 0 to fb.Framebuffer.height - 1 do
    let r = row_factor viewport ~row:y in
    let dn = r +. gf in
    if dn > 1e-9 then begin                        (* below the horizon: floor *)
      let d = (viewport.eye_z -. floor_base) /. dn in
      let wx = px +. (d *. dx) and wy = py +. (d *. dy) in
      (* The pattern is sampled at the world point, so it is anchored in the
         world rather than on the screen: its features foreshorten with
         distance and its rows tilt with the surface. *)
      let texel = Material.plane_texel room.floor.material ~x:wx ~y:wy in
      (* The same rule as the walls, without the orientation: a plane's normal
         is the same everywhere, so only the distance is left. *)
      let lit = Color.lerp texel air.Atmosphere.haze (1. -. Atmosphere.fog air d) in
      Framebuffer.set fb ~x:column ~y ~r:lit.Color.r ~g:lit.Color.g ~b:lit.Color.b
    end else (* above the horizon: ceiling (or sky, Step 4.4) *) ...
  done
]}

The ceiling is symmetric with its own plane and [denom < 0]. When both are in
view (only near the tilted horizon) pick the nearer positive [d].

Because walls now stand on the {e sloped} floor, compute each wall's [floor_z] and
[top_z] at its {b hit point} and cap [top_z] to the ceiling (if any) so a wall
never pokes through:

{@ocaml[
let hit_point = Vec.make (px +. d*.dx) (py +. d*.dy) in
let floor_z = Plane.elevation room.floor hit_point in
let top_z = match room.ceiling with
  | Room.Roof c -> Float.min (floor_z +. w.height) (Plane.elevation c.plane hit_point)
  | Room.Open _ -> floor_z +. w.height in   (* under sky it rises to its full height *)
]}

{2 Step 4.3 — Texturing the planes}

Sample the surface's [Texture] at the {b world point} the pixel casts to, tiling
every world unit, and blend it towards the haze by its distance — exactly the
walls' recipe, less the orientation. Anchoring the pattern
in world space is what makes the tilt read: its features foreshorten and their
rows tilt with the surface. This belongs on [Material], beside the wall lookup:

{@ocaml[
(* Material *)
let plane_texel t ~x ~y =
  let frac v = v -. Float.floor v in
  Texture.sample t.pattern ~u:(Texture.column_of_offset (frac x))
                           ~v:(Texture.column_of_offset (frac y))
]}

Use a checker for the floor and a bevelled panel for the ceiling, say — the same
two pattern functions at two colours.

{2 Step 4.4 — The sky}

[Room.Open] is the other half of the ceiling variant from Step 4.1. The sky is an
{b infinitely far backdrop}: its colour depends only on the {e direction looked
in} — the column's azimuth and how high up the pixel sits — never on where you
stand, so it does not slide as you walk, only wheels as you turn.

A sky is a value rather than a set of constants, because the ceiling holds one
{e per room}: two rooms either side of a doorway can stand under different skies.
[lib/sky.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type t = {
  horizon : Color.t; zenith : Color.t; sun : Color.t;
  sun_azimuth : float;   (* where the sun is around the compass *)
  sun_height : float;    (* 0 on the horizon, 1 overhead *)
  sun_radius : float;    (* how big and soft the glow is *)
  gradient : float;      (* how fast horizon gives way to zenith *)
}

let color t ~azimuth ~up =
  let k = Float.max 0. (Float.min 1. (up *. t.gradient)) in
  let sky = Color.lerp t.horizon t.zenith k in
  let daz = (* wrap azimuth - t.sun_azimuth to [-π,π] *) ... in
  let d = Float.hypot daz (up -. t.sun_height) in
  let glow = Float.max 0. (1. -. (d /. t.sun_radius)) in
  Color.lerp sky t.sun (glow *. glow)
]}

In the background pass, an above-horizon pixel in a room whose ceiling is
[Open sky] gets [Sky.color sky ~azimuth:(atan2 dir.y dir.x) ~up:(-. row_factor)].

{b The sky lights nothing.} What lights the walls is the [Atmosphere], which is
one per world. A room under a dusk sky is as brightly lit as one under noon
unless the air changes too — which is the right split, because the two are
genuinely independent.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 4.} A textured, tilted floor; overhead either a
textured ceiling or a gradient sky with a sun. Build a bigger level here — an
arena of pillars, low walls you see over — with [Room.path] and
[Room.regular_polygon] helpers (a polyline and a closed polygon of walls).

{{image:images/slopes.png}A tiled floor climbing the length of the hall to a doorway that stands higher than the floor underfoot, with the roof opening away from it as it goes}

{{image:images/daylight.png}The other case: a low wall under an open evening
sky, with a second, warmer sky visible through the doorway in it}

{b If the floor is a smear of one colour}, the pattern is being sampled in screen
space rather than at the world point. {b If the horizon has a bright seam}, a
pixel is being cast against both planes and the wrong [d] is winning: take the
nearer {e positive} one.

{1 Phase 5 — Camera & window}

{2 Step 5.1 — Mouse look and looking up/down}

{b Yaw} already rotates [dir]/[right]. {b Pitch} is faked by {e shearing} the whole
image vertically — a raycaster has no true vertical rotation (that would tilt the
walls). We already put pitch into the [horizon]: [horizon = height/2 +
pitch·height]. Everything (the plane casts, every wall) is measured from
[horizon], so moving it looks up or down for free. Clamp pitch to
[Config.max_pitch].

{@ocaml[
(* Player *)
let pitch_by p ~radians =
  let l = Config.max_pitch in
  { p with pitch = Float.max (-.l) (Float.min l (p.pitch +. radians)) }
]}

Input ([lib/input.ml]) reads held keys as a snapshot and blends in {b relative
mouse} motion. Each field comes out as a finished per-frame delta. Held keys ask
for a {e speed} — [Config.move_speed] is cells per second, [turn_speed] radians per
second — which [dt], the length of the frame, turns into the distance covered
this frame; that is what keeps walking speed independent of the frame rate. Mouse
deltas are already everything since the last frame, so they are not scaled:

{@ocaml[
let mouse_delta () = let _, (dx, dy) = Sdl.get_relative_mouse_state () in
  (float_of_int dx, float_of_int dy)

type motion = { forward : float; strafe : float; turn : float; pitch : float }

let motion ~dt =
  let keys = Sdl.get_keyboard_state () in
  let down sc = Bigarray.Array1.get keys sc = 1 in
  let axis ~speed ~pos ~neg =
    ((if List.exists down pos then 1. else 0.)
     -. (if List.exists down neg then 1. else 0.)) *. speed *. dt in
  let mdx, mdy = mouse_delta () in
  { forward = axis ~speed:Config.move_speed ~pos:Sdl.Scancode.[w] ~neg:Sdl.Scancode.[s];
    strafe  = axis ~speed:Config.move_speed ~pos:Sdl.Scancode.[d] ~neg:Sdl.Scancode.[a];
    turn = axis ~speed:Config.turn_speed ~pos:Sdl.Scancode.[right] ~neg:Sdl.Scancode.[left]
           +. (mdx *. Config.look_sensitivity);
    (* mouse up is negative dy but should look up, hence the minus *)
    pitch = axis ~speed:Config.pitch_speed ~pos:Sdl.Scancode.[up] ~neg:Sdl.Scancode.[down]
            -. (mdy *. Config.pitch_sensitivity) }
]}

Enable relative mouse mode once at startup ([Sdl.set_relative_mouse_mode true])
so the cursor is hidden, pinned, and never hits a screen edge. Turn text input
off in the same breath ([Sdl.stop_text_input ()]): SDL switches it on for you
when video starts, on the assumption that a program wanting characters should
not have to ask twice. A window left as a live text client is one where macOS
answers a held key with a menu of accented letters instead of repeating it —
which is not what a game holding [w] to walk forward wants. The engine [step]
applies the deltas in order — turn, pitch, then walk:

{@ocaml[
let step world player (m : Input.motion) =
  player |> Player.turn ~radians:m.turn |> Player.pitch_by ~radians:m.pitch
         |> Player.walk world ~forward:m.forward ~strafe:m.strafe
]}

{b What the shipped engine does instead.} Everything above is right except for
one thing: it decides which keys walk. That is a decision the engine has no
business making, so [motion] is split in two. [Input] keeps the reading — the
keyboard, the mouse, the edges and the hold timer — and {!Camlcast.Binding} holds
a {e table} the game supplies, from controls and analog sources onto the four
axes. [Binding.motion] is the [axis] function above with its keys taken out of
it, and because it reads a frame of input rather than SDL, it is pure and can be
tested without a window. It is also where a gamepad would arrive: a stick is a
term on an axis like a key is, differing only in that it reports a fraction of
full speed rather than all or nothing.

Build it the way it is above first. The split is worth making once there is
something to walk around in, and worth understanding as a split rather than as
the way input has to look.

{2 Step 5.2 — Resizing and fullscreen}

Cache {b no} size. Each frame, ask SDL for the current drawable size and build a
fresh [Viewport] from it — that alone handles resizing, HiDPI and the fullscreen
transition, with no resize events to handle. Because per-pixel casting costs one
division per pixel, render into an {b internal buffer capped} to
[Config.max_render_height] and let the GPU scale it up, keeping the frame rate
steady at any window size:

{@ocaml[
let internal_size ~width ~height =
  let scale = Int.max 1 ((height + Config.max_render_height - 1) / Config.max_render_height) in
  (Int.max 1 (width / scale), Int.max 1 (height / scale))
]}

Fullscreen is a toggle read from the event queue (F11); use
[fullscreen_desktop], which stretches over the desktop instantly and needs
nothing restored. The renderer notices the new size next frame on its own.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 5.} Mouse look with pitch; the window resizes and goes
fullscreen smoothly, and dragging it wider shows more of the room to the sides
rather than magnifying what was already there.

That last claim is the one worth looking at twice. Both of these are the same
room from the same spot, and the only difference between them is the shape of
the window:

{{image:images/hor-narrow.png}A square window: one green wall and the corner of
a post}

{{image:images/hor-wide.png}A wide window from the same spot: the same green
wall at the same size, with a red wall and a blue one now visible to either
side}

Nothing grew. The vertical field of view is fixed, so the second window did not
magnify the first — it revealed what was always off to the sides of it.

{b If the walls lean as you look up}, pitch is being applied as a rotation
somewhere rather than purely as a shear of the horizon. {b If the view stretches
when the window changes shape}, the projection is being taken from the width;
anchor it to the height.

{1 Phase 6 — Props: transparency, decals, sprites}

These three sit {e on top of} the opaque world and need careful ordering. The key
enabling idea is a {b depth buffer}.

{2 Step 6.1 — A per-pixel depth buffer}

To know whether a sprite or a see-through wall is hidden by an opaque wall, we
need the distance of the nearest opaque wall {b per pixel} — not per column,
because a {e short} wall only covers the lower part of its column; anything behind
it should still show its top.

We already added [depth : float array] to the framebuffer and [clear_depth]. When
drawing an {b opaque} wall, record its distance at each pixel it covers:

{@ocaml[
(* in the wall loop, occlude = true for opaque walls *)
let index = (y * width) + column in
Framebuffer.set fb ~x:column ~y ~r:cr ~g:cg ~b:cb;
if occlude then depth.(index) <- d
]}

Opaque walls are painted far-to-near, so a nearer one overwrites both colour and
depth; the final [depth.(index)] is the nearest opaque wall covering that pixel
(or [infinity] where only background shows).

{2 Step 6.2 — Transparent walls}

Give [Texture] a per-texel {b alpha} and an [opaque] flag; add
[generate_masked f] where [f] returns {e (brightness, alpha)}, and patterns like a
grille (solid bars, clear gaps) and glass (translucent panes):

{@ocaml[
let bars = generate_masked (fun ~u ~v ->
  if u mod 16 < 5 || v mod 16 < 5 then (80, 255) else (0, 0))     (* holes *)
let glass = generate_masked (fun ~u ~v ->
  let mullion = u<2||u>=size-2||v<2||v>=size-2||abs(u-size/2)<2||abs(v-size/2)<2 in
  if mullion then (150, 255) else (235, 80))                       (* translucent panes *)
]}

Framebuffer gains an alpha blend ([src·a + dst·(255−a)] per channel):

{@ocaml[
let blend t ~x ~y ~r ~g ~b ~a =
  let i = ((y*t.width)+x)*4 and p = t.pixels in
  let inv = 255 - a in
  let mix dst src = ((src*a) + (dst*inv)) / 255 in
  Bigarray.Array1.(unsafe_set p i (mix (unsafe_get p i) b));
  Bigarray.Array1.(unsafe_set p (i+1) (mix (unsafe_get p (i+1)) g));
  Bigarray.Array1.(unsafe_set p (i+2) (mix (unsafe_get p (i+2)) r))
]}

In the wall loop, a texel with [a = 255] is written (and records depth), [a = 0]
is skipped (a hole), otherwise it is blended. See-through walls {b don't} occlude
(don't write depth); they are collected and drawn {e after} the opaque pass so
whatever is behind them is already on screen to show through.

{2 Step 6.3 — Decals (pictures on walls)}

Decals are colourful, so they use a new {b RGBA [Image]} type (full colour +
alpha, unlike greyscale [Texture]) — a framed painting, a poster. Generate one
in code as below, or read one with [Image.load]. It is a rectangle rather than
a square, because a poster is: [height] defaults to [width] when you do not say.
[lib/image.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type t = { width : int; height : int; pixels : Color.t array; alpha : int array }
let make ?height width f =
  let height = Option.value height ~default:width in
  let n = width*height in
  let pixels = Array.make n (Color.rgb 0 0 0) and alpha = Array.make n 0 in
  for v = 0 to height-1 do for u = 0 to width-1 do
    let c, a = f ~u ~v in let i = v*width+u in pixels.(i) <- c; alpha.(i) <- a
  done done; { size; pixels; alpha }
let index t ~u ~v = (v * t.size) + u
]}

A wall carries a [decals] list — each decal a picture at a position [along] the
wall and height [z], with half-extents, on one [facing] {b face} of it. Since
[hit.along] and the face are both constant down a column, decide once which
decals this column falls in (and their texture column), then per pixel test the
height range and {b blend} the image texel over the wall, in the same light as
the wall.

Which face the viewer is on is the sign of [(eye − wall.a) · wall.normal], and
since [perp] is a quarter turn to the left and rooms are wound counter-clockwise,
the normal points {e into} the room. Put that test in the once-per-column half
rather than the per-pixel one, and put it somewhere the picker can call too — a
mark you can see and a mark you can aim at must be the same mark, and the surest
way to keep them the same is for there to be one function that says so.

{2 Step 6.4 — Sprites (billboards)}

A sprite is an [Image] standing in the world that always faces the player. Add
[sprites : sprite array] to the world ([{ pos; base; size; image }], where [base]
is how far its foot floats above the floor — [0.] for something on the ground).
To draw one:

{ol
{li Camera-space coordinates: [depth_s = (pos − player.pos) · dir] (perpendicular distance) and [lateral = (pos − player.pos) · right]. Skip if [depth_s ≤ 0] (behind you).}
{li Screen column of the centre — invert the camera-plane mapping: [camera_x = lateral / (depth_s · half_width)], then [center = (camera_x + 1) · width / 2].}
{li Vertical placement: project the foot at [floor_z + base] and the top a [size] above that, with [project_height]. Measure [base] from the floor rather than absolutely and a sprite rides a slope instead of the ground climbing through it — the same rule a decal's [z] follows. The horizontal extent is [center ± (y_base − y_top)/2 · aspect], where [aspect] is the image's own [width / height]: taking it from the picture is what stops a wide one being squashed into a square, and leaves a square one exactly as it was.}
{li For each pixel, sample the image; draw it (blended) {b only where [depth_s < depth.(pixel)]} — nearer than the opaque wall there. Per-pixel is what lets a short wall hide only the sprite's legs, not its head.}
}

{@ocaml[
let camera_x = lateral /. (depth_s *. viewport.half_width) in
let center = (camera_x +. 1.) *. float_of_int width /. 2. in
let foot = floor_z +. s.base in
let y_base = project_height viewport ~z:foot ~distance:depth_s in
let y_top  = project_height viewport ~z:(foot +. s.size) ~distance:depth_s in
(* loop cols in [center-half, center+half], rows in [y_top, y_base]; blend where
   depth_s < depth.((y*width)+col) and the image alpha > 0, each texel carried
   towards the haze by fog depth_s — one distance for the whole billboard, so
   that blend's constant half is worked out once outside the loops *)
]}

{2 Step 6.5 — Compositing order}

Sprites and see-through walls are {e both} translucent, so they can't each own a
pass: a sprite in front of a window must cover it, one behind must show through
it. Put them in {b one list, sorted by depth, drawn farthest-first}, each still
tested per pixel against the opaque depth buffer:

{@ocaml[
type fragment = Sprite of Room.sprite | See_through of int * Vec.t * Ray.hit

(* build (depth, fragment) list from sprites and deferred see-through hits,
   sort by depth descending, then: *)
List.iter (fun (d, f) -> match f with
  | Sprite s               -> draw_sprite fb viewport room player s ~depth_s:d
  | See_through (col,dir,h) -> draw_wall fb viewport room player ~column:col ~dir ~occlude:false h)
  ordered
]}

So a whole frame is: {b backgrounds + opaque walls per column} (recording
per-pixel depth), then {b one combined translucent pass} of sprites and windows.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 6.} A wall hung with a painting and a poster; a grille
and a window you see the room through; barrels and figures standing about,
correctly occluded — a short wall hides only their lower halves.

{{image:images/gallery.png}Two pictures hung on the far wall, with figures
standing between them and the eye — the nearer one covering part of a frame}

{{image:images/glass.png}The same room through a grille and a tinted window:
both write colour and neither writes depth}

{b If a sprite shows through the wall in front of it}, the depth test is
per-column rather than per-pixel. {b If a sprite behind a window vanishes}, the
window wrote depth: see-through things must not.

{1 Phase 7 — Rooms and doorways}

Everything so far draws {e one} room. This phase makes a world out of many, and
it is the phase that separates this engine from a Wolfenstein-style grid: a world
is a {b graph} of rooms, each authored in its own coordinate frame, joined at
doorways that you both see through and walk through.

The surprise is how little machinery that takes. There is no global coordinate
system, no scene graph and no transform stack — just a rigid motion per link,
derived from four points.

{2 Step 7.1 — Rigid motions between frames}

A room knows nothing of where it sits. What relates two of them is the doorway
they share: the {e same opening}, described twice, once in each room's
coordinates. So a link is the rigid motion carrying one description onto the
other. [lib/transform.ml]:

{@ocaml[
type t = { cos : float; sin : float; offset : Vec.t }   (* rotate, then translate *)

let identity = { cos = 1.; sin = 0.; offset = Vec.make 0. 0. }

let direction t (v : Vec.t) =
  Vec.make ((t.cos *. v.x) -. (t.sin *. v.y)) ((t.sin *. v.x) +. (t.cos *. v.y))

let point t v = Vec.add (direction t v) t.offset
]}

{b Why the endpoints reverse.} Given segment [a1..a2] in one room and [b1..b2] in
the other, the transform maps [a1 ↦ b2] and [a2 ↦ b1] — {e crossed}, not
straight. The two rooms describe the same opening from opposite sides, so walking
each room's boundary through the doorway traverses it in opposite directions. Map
them straight across and the neighbour arrives mirrored, which reads as a room
turned inside out.

{@ocaml[
let between ~a1 ~a2 ~b1 ~b2 =
  let u = Vec.sub a2 a1 and v = Vec.sub b1 b2 in    (* note: b1 - b2 *)
  let lu = Vec.length u in
  let c = ((u.x *. v.x) +. (u.y *. v.y)) /. (lu *. lu)
  and s = ((u.x *. v.y) -. (u.y *. v.x)) /. (lu *. lu) in
  let t = { cos = c; sin = s; offset = Vec.make 0. 0. } in
  { t with offset = Vec.sub b2 (direction t a1) }
]}

{b The authoring rule this creates}, and the one mistake everybody makes once:
give a doorway's endpoints in the {b same winding direction as the room's
boundary walls}. Wind one the other way and every ray and every step through it
arrives mirrored.

{2 Step 7.2 — A doorway is a gap you cut}

A doorway is a {e threshold}: a segment in the room's boundary that is not a
wall. The wall it replaces still has to exist either side of it, and the strip
above it — the {b lintel} — still has to be drawn, or you see over the top of a
closed door.

The way to keep a boundary and its openings honest about each other is to never
author them separately. Cut the doorway {e out of} the wall and return both:

{@ocaml[
type lintel = { top : float; material : Material.t }

type threshold = {
  name : string;            (* what a link refers to it by *)
  a : Vec.t; b : Vec.t;
  height : float;           (* how tall the opening is *)
  lintel : lintel option;   (* the wall left above it *)
  edge : Vec.t; length : float; normal : Vec.t;
}

(* The two jambs left either side of a gap [width] wide in the middle of the
   wall that would have run a..b, and the threshold filling that gap. *)
let doorway ~name ~width ~opening ~height ~material a b =
  let edge = Vec.sub b a in
  let span = Vec.length edge in
  if not (span > 0.) then invalid_arg ("Room.doorway: no wall to cut: " ^ name);
  if not (width > 0.) then invalid_arg ("Room.doorway: no width: " ^ name);
  if not (width <= span) then invalid_arg ("Room.doorway: wider than the wall: " ^ name);
  let half = Vec.scale edge (width /. (2. *. span)) in
  let middle = Vec.scale (Vec.add a b) 0.5 in
  let p = Vec.sub middle half and q = Vec.add middle half in
  ([ wall ~height ~material a p; wall ~height ~material q b ],
   threshold ~name ~height:opening ~lintel:{ top = height; material } p q)
]}

Note the three refusals, and that each is written as the {e negation} of the
passing condition. A [nan] argument answers [false] to every ordered comparison,
so [not (span > 0.)] catches it where [span <= 0.] would wave it through — and a
threshold whose coordinates are all [nan] is accepted by everything downstream
and then draws nothing, which is a miserable bug to find.

{2 Step 7.3 — A world of rooms}

Now the graph. Rooms have names, links name a doorway on each side, and the world
resolves both into indices once, at construction:

{@ocaml[
type portal = {
  threshold : Room.threshold;
  to_room : int;        (* which room is through it *)
  twin : int;           (* which of that room's thresholds is its other side *)
  onto : Transform.t;   (* how to carry a pose or a ray across *)
}

type t = {
  rooms : Room.t array;
  names : string array;
  portals : portal option array array;   (* per room, per threshold *)
  atmosphere : Atmosphere.t;
  spawn : location;
}

let make ~rooms ~links ~atmosphere ~spawn = ...
]}

[make] is where an unplayable level should die, and it should die loudly —
[invalid_arg], not a result, because these are bugs in the level rather than
conditions to handle. Refuse: an unknown room or doorway name; two rooms with the
same name; two doorways with the same name in one room; a zero-length threshold;
a doorway linked twice; a doorway nothing links to; and linked doorways that
differ in length or height. Every one of those makes a link meaningless, and each
is far cheaper to catch here than to recognise from the wrong picture later.

{2 Step 7.4 — Drawing through a doorway}

An open threshold is a {b portal}: instead of drawing a surface, the renderer
draws {e the neighbouring room}, seen from the transformed camera, clipped to the
opening.

Which is less work than it sounds, because the per-column structure already fits.
A threshold is intersected by exactly the same ray test as a wall — it has the
same [edge], [length] and [normal] for exactly that reason — so it arrives in the
same farthest-first sorted stream. When one comes up:

{ol
{li carry the ray's origin and direction through [portal.onto] into the
    neighbour's frame, keeping the distance the doorway was met at;}
{li work out which rows of this column the opening covers, and pass them down as
    a mask;}
{li recurse, drawing that room into those rows only, and drawing nothing nearer
    than that distance;}
{li stop at [Config.max_portal_depth] and fill whatever is left with haze.}}

The distance carried in the first step is the one thing here that is easy to
leave out and hard to see the need for. The transform lands the camera {e behind}
the neighbour's own copy of the opening, so a ray cast from there meets anything
that room has standing on the near side of its own doorway {e before} it reaches
the doorway at all. In a convex room there is never anything there. In one that
folds back on itself — a doorway set at the back of a recess — there is, and it
is in the space the player is really standing in rather than in the picture the
doorway shows; drawn, it covers the room it was supposed to be a window onto.

A single number is enough, and is exact rather than approximate: along one ray,
which side of the threshold's line a point falls on varies linearly with the
distance and changes sign exactly where the ray crosses it. Nothing accumulates
either, since a rigid motion preserves distance and each recursion's cut-off is
an opening that already cleared the last one. {!Camlcast.Sight} cuts its ray at
the same distance by the same rule, which is what keeps what the crosshair can
pick the same as what the frame draws.

The depth cap is what makes a {e cyclic} room graph terminate — and rooms may
absolutely be cyclic, since nothing checks that the geometry is consistent. Join
a room to one four doorways behind it and the corridor returns you somewhere it
could not possibly go; the renderer neither notices nor minds.

Keeping the mask {b per column} rather than as a rectangle is what clips the view
to the doorway's actual shape — which matters the moment a doorway is seen at an
angle, or through another doorway.

{2 Step 7.5 — Walking through one}

A doorway is a {e gap} in the boundary, so nothing in this room stops a step
taken through it. That has two consequences, and missing either one is a bug you
will meet within a minute of playing.

{b Collision must ask the neighbour.} A step that comes near a threshold has to
be carried into the neighbour's frame and tested there too — otherwise you clip
through the far side's wall while straddling the opening. So [passable] moves up
from [Room] to [World], and [Player.slide] and [Player.walk] take a world from
here on. [Room.passable] stays, as the single-room question [World.passable] asks
twice.

Only the part of the step that reaches the neighbour, though — cut where it
crosses the threshold's plane, with the collision padding of slack because the
player is a disc. This is the same trap as the distance in Step 7.4, wearing
different clothes: a room's walls answer for the space that room encloses and no
other, and a room that folds back on itself has walls of its own on {e this} side
of its own doorway. Handed the whole step, one of them refuses a clear stride
towards an open door from right across the room.

Once a step can report the doorways it went through, [Player.walk] has a choice
to make about them, and the shipped engine resolves it by not having a [walk] at
all: {!Camlcast.Player.traverse} returns the pose {e and} the crossings, and
{!Camlcast.Engine.step} is the one place that drops them. One function that
answers everything and one caller that asks for less beats two functions that
have to be kept in step.

{b The pose has to cross mid-step.} Movement resolves one axis at a time, so
whichever leg goes through the doorway carries the pose into the next room before
the other leg is taken. A diagonal through an opening then finishes in the room
it ended up in, checked against {e that} room's walls.

Report the crossings rather than inferring them:

{@ocaml[
type crossing = {
  from_room : int; from_threshold : int;
  to_room : int; to_threshold : int;
  onto : Transform.t;
}

type movement = { player : t; crossings : crossing list }
]}

A single frame can cross more than one doorway — round a jamb, out through an
opening and back in through its twin, or all the way round a loop of rooms and
back where it started. Comparing the room index before and after calls every one
of those nothing happening, which is why the list exists.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 7.} Two rooms and a doorway. Standing in one, you can
see into the other through the opening and nowhere else; walking through, nothing
catches at the threshold; and turning round, the room you came from is where you
left it. Build the same room twice and join it in two places: both copies are one
value, and only the doorways put them anywhere.

{{image:images/portals.png}Two doorways in one wall, and through both of them
the same red room with the same figure standing in it}

{b If the neighbour looks mirrored}, the threshold endpoints are wound against
the boundary. {b If you can walk through the far wall while standing in the
opening}, collision is not asking the neighbour. {b If the view through a doorway
is a rectangle rather than the doorway's shape}, the mask is not per column.

{1 Phase 8 — Assembly}

{2 Step 8.1 — The engine loop and resource safety}

Nest [with_resource] so SDL init, the window, the renderer, relative mouse mode
and the framebuffer are all released in reverse order even on error. The loop
carries the immutable [player], the [fullscreen] flag and the time the previous
frame started between frames.

{b What the shipped engine does instead.} The nesting is the same, but it ends
in a {!Camlcast.Engine.window} handed to a function rather than in the loop
itself — {!Camlcast.Engine.with_window}, with the loop a separate call played on
what it opens. That is what lets a launcher show a menu, run a demo and show the
menu again without the window disappearing twice on the way. The bracket is the
point and survives the split: a window is still acquired, used and released in
one expression, and still released on an error or an exception. What changes is
that the flags the loop carried — [fullscreen], and whether the mouse is
captured — move onto the window, because they now outlive any one run and SDL
will not answer for either of them.

Pace the loop by measuring, not by assuming: sleep
only what is {e left} of [Config.frame_budget], and feed the real frame length to
[Input.motion] as [dt]. Sleep a flat 16 ms after a 10 ms frame and you get 38 FPS
and a player who walks two thirds as fast as intended:

{@ocaml[
let seconds () =                       (* SDL's high resolution counter *)
  Int64.to_float (Sdl.get_performance_counter ())
  /. Int64.to_float (Sdl.get_performance_frequency ())

(* Cap a frame that the machine, not the world, made long — a dragged window, a
   stall — or one step could leap the player clean over a wall. *)
let frame_time ~previous ~now =
  Float.min Config.max_frame_time (Float.max 0. (now -. previous))

let idle_time ~spent = Float.max 0. (Config.frame_budget -. spent)

let rec loop ctx ~player ~fullscreen ~previous =
  (* Drains the event queue — which has to happen every frame or the window
     stops responding — and says whether the desktop asked us to stop. *)
  if Input.quit_requested ctx.event then Ok ()
  else
    let* fullscreen =
      if Input.pressed ctx.actions (Input.Key Key.f11) then
        set_fullscreen ctx.window (not fullscreen)
      else Ok fullscreen in
    let now = seconds () in
    let player = step ctx.world player (Input.motion ~dt:(frame_time ~previous ~now)) in
    let* () = Renderer.render ctx.renderer ctx.framebuffer ctx.world player in
    Sdl.delay (Int32.of_float (idle_time ~spent:(seconds () -. now) *. 1000.));
    (* Timed start to start, so the sleep counts towards the next frame. *)
    loop ctx ~player ~fullscreen ~previous:now
]}

[Renderer.render] reads the output size, resizes the framebuffer to the internal
size if needed, [clear_depth], [draw_frame], [present], [render_present].

The three above the loop are the only part of it that never touches a window, so
in the shipped engine they live apart from one, as {!Camlcast.Clock} —
[frame_time] and [idle_time] under those names, and [seconds] as
{!Camlcast.Clock.now}. That is the whole of what a test can pin about pacing, and
splitting it out is what lets {!Camlcast.Engine} keep an interface with nothing
in it but a window to play on and the two ways into the loop.

{b Checkpoint — Milestone 8, the whole engine.} Everything runs together.

{{image:images/showcase.png}The showcase plaza: a twelve-sided ring open to
the sky, with a brick pillar, a barrel, a figure, a low wall, a leaded window
and a steel grille all in one frame}

{2 Step 8.2 — Tests and docs}

Keep the pure logic testable and test it with {b Alcotest} — one executable per
module, sharing fixtures (a hand-checkable 4×4 room). Good things to pin:

{ul
{li [Vec]: cross is zero for parallel vectors; [normalize] is unit length.}
{li [Ray]: from the room centre every wall is distance 2; a diagonal is [2] in {e units of direction} (the no-fish-eye property); hits come back farthest-first.}
{li [Plane]: on a flat floor one below the eye, [view_distance] reduces to [eye_z / row_factor]; the floor is only in view below the horizon.}
{li [Viewport]: a point at eye height projects onto the horizon at every distance; [half_width = tan(fov/2)] at the reference shape; widening grows [half_width] but not [projection] (Hor+).}
{li [Sky]: the zenith is bluer and darker than the horizon; the sun is a bright spot; azimuth wraps at ±π.}
}

[Input], [Framebuffer] and [Renderer] need a live SDL surface, so test their
{e pure} pieces (in [Plane], [Viewport], [Palette], [Sky], [Image]) instead.

Document modules with [(** ... *)] and build HTML with [odoc] ([dune build @doc];
the library needs a [public_name]). Put derivations in the docstrings.

{b How to verify rendering without a screen.} SDL can render offscreen: create a
hidden window + renderer, fill a [Framebuffer] with [draw_frame], wrap its bytes
in a surface ([Sdl.create_rgb_surface_from], masks for ARGB8888), and
[save_bmp]. Eyeballing four viewpoints catches sign errors no unit test will.

{1 Appendix}

{2 Module dependency map}

Each depends only on those above it (dune figures the order out; this is the
mental model):

{[
Config       (every tunable constant)
Key          (the keyboard, named: a place on the board, not a letter)
Result_ext   (let* / let+, and acquire/use/release)
Vec          (2-D vectors, with the dot and cross products)
Transform    (rigid motions between room-local frames)
Plane        (inclined floor/ceiling + the casting equation)
Color        (8-bit RGB, shading, blending, level)
Texture      (colour surface patterns + per-texel alpha, and wrapping noise)
Material     (what a surface is made of: its pattern)
Atmosphere   (the air: fog, haze, and where the light comes from)
Sky          (directional gradient + sun, one per room)
Image        (RGBA pictures for decals and sprites)
Room         (wall segments + decals, thresholds, floor, ceiling or sky, sprites, collision)
World        (named rooms, linked portals, the world's air and spawn)
Ray          (ray-vs-segment; every wall crossed, farthest first)
Player       (pose pos/dir/right/pitch; movement, sliding and crossings)
Viewport     (window → projection, eye height, pitch shear, resize rules)
Input        (keyboard + mouse as they are: controls, edges, holds, analog)
Binding      (what those controls are for — the game's table, read purely)
Framebuffer  (CPU pixels + alpha blend + per-pixel depth + streaming texture)
Renderer     (floor/ceiling/sky, opaque walls + decals, then sprites + windows by depth)
Engine       (window lifetime, fullscreen, the loop played on one)
]}

The shipped engine adds six more that this tutorial does not build, because none
of them is needed to get a picture on screen: [Surface] (decoding a PNG or
JPEG), [Asset] (finding a file relative to the executable), [Door] (a leaf hung
in a doorway), [Sight] (what the crosshair is on), [Paint] (clipped shapes over a
finished frame) and [Font] (a bitmap font on a fixed grid).
{{!page-"making-a-game"} Making a game on CamlCast} covers all of them from the
using end.

{2 Constants worth exposing ([Config])}

[fov], [reference_aspect], [eye_height] (0.5), [move_speed] (~3.6 cells/s),
[turn_speed] (~2.1 rad/s), [collision_padding] (~0.15),
[look_sensitivity]/[pitch_sensitivity] (~0.0025), [pitch_speed] (~1.2/s),
[max_pitch] (~0.75), [max_render_height] (~480), [frame_budget] (1/60 s),
[max_frame_time] (~0.1 s), and — once Phase 7 exists — [max_portal_depth] (3).

Note what is {e not} here. [fog_distance] and [min_brightness] started life as
constants and belong on the [Atmosphere] instead: they are properties of a place,
not of the program, and a world that cannot change its own weather is a world
where every room looks the same.

{2 Hard-won gotchas}

{ul
{li {b [update_texture] pitch is bytes for a streaming texture, elements for a static one.} [render_read_pixels] is bytes. A wrong pitch squashes/stripes.}
{li {b Never read the back buffer after [render_present]} — it's undefined. Render offscreen for screenshots.}
{li {b Opaque surfaces:} set the alpha byte to 255 or your captures come out blank.}
{li {b Use [floor], not [int_of_float], to find a cell} if you ever add a grid — truncation folds cells −1 and 0 together. (We use segments and sidestep this.)}
{li {b Collision must sweep the {e path}, not just test the endpoint}, or a fast step tunnels through a thin wall — or slips past the end of one and lands clear.}
{li {b Never scale motion per frame and then sleep a fixed 16 ms.} The sleep is {e on top of} the render, so speed follows the frame rate. Measure the frame.}
{li {b [horizon] is a float}; only convert to an int pixel at the last moment.}
{li {b Sprites vs transparent walls need one depth-sorted pass}, and occlusion must be {b per pixel}, or short walls cull whole sprites behind them.}
}

{2 Exercises to go further}

{ul
{li {b Wall tops.} Floor-cast the horizontal top faces of short walls so you see them when looking down.}
{li {b Textured sky} by sampling a panoramic image by azimuth/elevation.}
{li {b Directional sprites} by choosing the image from the angle between the sprite's facing and the view. (Animating one is already there: build the pictures once, then hand [Room.with_sprites] the same room with a different frame in it — [demo/floating.ml].)}
{li {b Coloured lighting} by tinting fog per region, or point lights that dim with distance from a source.}
{li {b A minimap} drawing the wall segments and the player from above.}
{li {b Per-pixel depth for translucent interpenetration} (splitting a window where a sprite passes through it) if you want fully correct transparency.}
}

{1 Where to go next}

You have an engine. The other page —
{{!page-"making-a-game"} Making a game on CamlCast} — is about what to do with
one: materials, doors, sprites you can pick up, a HUD, a crosshair that knows
what it is pointing at, and a level that builds itself as it is walked into.

The modules of this repository carry the derivations at full length in their own
docstrings, and are worth reading against what you have just written: {!Camlcast.Ray}
for the ray-versus-segment algebra, {!Camlcast.Plane} for the casting equation,
{!Camlcast.Viewport} for the projection and the resize rules,
{!Camlcast.Transform} for why linked endpoints reverse, and
{!Camlcast.World} for the portal machinery.

Built in the same spirit as the engine it describes: small modules, derivations
written down, and a picture on screen at the end of every phase. Happy casting.
